# Line endings.
#
# Without this, a checkout on Windows converts shell scripts to CRLF, and a CRLF shebang is fatal:
# the kernel reads the interpreter as "/usr/bin/env bash\r", which does not exist, and the script
# dies with "bad interpreter: No such file or directory". The file looks perfectly fine in an editor.
#
# Batch files need the opposite: cmd.exe mishandles parenthesised blocks in LF-only .cmd files, so
# those are pinned to CRLF.

* text=auto

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# The shell scripts carry no extension by convention, so the rule above does not reach them and they
# would fall back to `* text=auto` - which on Windows means CRLF, which means a broken shebang:
# the kernel reads the interpreter as "/usr/bin/env bash" and the file looks perfectly fine in an
# editor. Every extensionless script has to be listed here by name.
build         text eol=lf
polson        text eol=lf
polson_webapp text eol=lf
polson_run    text eol=lf

*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Generated dependency lock files: exact bytes matter for review, and a merge tool guessing at them
# helps nobody. Treat conflicts as something a person resolves by recompiling.
requirements.txt    -merge
packages.lock.json  -merge

# Rendered artifacts and reference material are binary; never line-ending-convert or diff them.
*.webp binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.pdf  binary
